#comment("\n\
The file is automatically generated
by [atsdoc] from CHAP_REFERENCE/main.atxt.\n\
")
#comment("\n\
Time of Generation: #timestamp()\
")
<chapter
id="reference"
>#title("References")

#para("\

A reference is a singleton array, that is, an array of size 1.  It is
persistent in the sense that it can be freely copied and the memory for
storing its content is never freed: the type of a reference is not linear,
so there is no way to tell when the last copy of a reference is gone.
Therefore, a reference cannot be allocated on heap in this dialect of ATS2,
which has no garbage collector (GC): the function that used to do so
(#dyncode("ref_make_elt"), also spelled #dyncode("ref")) was removed. What
is left is the possibility of forming a reference from memory that is
never freed, for instance, a variable that is declared at the toplevel.

")

#para("\

Given a viewtype VT, the type for references to values of viewtype VT is
#stacode("ref")(VT). For convenience, the type constructor #stacode("ref")
is declared to be abstract in ATS (it is a pointer at run-time). The
following cast-function shows what the type stands for: a reference is a
pointer together with a #emphasis("vbox") that allows the content of the
memory at the pointer to be accessed at any time.

#sats2xhtml('\
castfn ref_get_viewptr
  {a:vt0p} (r: ref a):<> [l:agz] (vbox (a @ l) | ptr l)
// end of [ref_get_viewptr]
')

The interfaces for various functions on references can be found in
#myatscodelink("prelude/SATS/reference.sats", "prelude/SATS/reference.sats").\

")#comment("para")

#para("\
For creating a reference, the cast-function #dyncode("ref_make_viewptr")
of the following interface can be called:

#sats2xhtml('\
castfn
ref_make_viewptr
  {a:vt0p}{l:addr}
  (pf: a @ l | p: ptr(l)):<> ref(a)
// end of [ref_make_viewptr]
')

It takes a pointer and a proof of the at-view associated with the pointer
and returns a reference after consuming the proof. As
#dyncode("ref_make_viewptr") is a cast-function, it causes no run-time
overhead. The memory of the reference is the memory of the pointer; it must
never be freed, which is the case, for instance, for a variable that is
declared at the toplevel (that is, a statically allocated variable).

")

#para("\

For reading from and writing through a reference, the function
templates #dyncode("ref_get_elt") and #dyncode("ref_set_elt") can be used,
respectively, which are assigned the following types:

#sats2xhtml('\
fun{a:t0p}
ref_get_elt(r: ref a):<!ref> (a)
fun{a:t0p}
ref_set_elt(r: ref a, x: a):<!refwrt> void
')

Note that the symbol #stacode("!ref") indicates that the so-called
ref-effect may occur when #dyncode("ref_get_elt") is evaluated. Similarly,
#stacode("!refwrt") means both ref-effect and wrt-effect may occur when
#dyncode("ref_set_elt").  Given a reference #dyncode("r") and a value
#dyncode("v"), #dyncode("ref_get_elt(r)") and #dyncode("ref_set_elt(r, v)")
can be written as #dyncode("!r") and #dyncode("!r := v"), respectively, and
can also be written as #dyncode("r[]") and #dyncode("r[] := v"),
respectively, in terms of bracket-notation.\

")#comment("para")

#para("\

A reference is typically employed to record some form of persistent state.
For instance, following is such an example, where the reference
#dyncode("count") is made from the toplevel variable
#dyncode("theCount"), which is local to the example:

#dats2xhtml('\
local
//
var theCount: int = 0
//
val count = ref_make_viewptr{int} (view@(theCount) | addr@(theCount))
//
in (* in of [local] *)

fun genNewName
  (prfx: string): Strptr0 = let
  val n = !count
  val () = !count := n + 1
  val s1 = string0_copy(prfx)
  val s2 = tostrptr_int(n)
  val res = strptr_append(s1, s2)
  val () = strptr_free(s1)
  val () = strptr_free(s2)
in
  res
end // end of [genNewName]

end // end of [local]
')

The function #dyncode("genNewName") is called to generate fresh names. As
the integer content of the reference #dyncode("count") is updated whenever
a call to #dyncode("genNewName") is made, each name returned by
#dyncode("genNewName") is guaranteed to have not been generated
before. The names are linear strings, and they are freed by the caller.

")#comment("para")

#para("\

#emphasis("Misuse of References")
References are commonly misused in practice. The following program is often
written by a beginner of functional programming who has already learned
(some) imperative programming:

#atscode('\
fun fact
  (n: int): int = let
  val res = ref<int> (1)
  fun loop (n: int):<cloref1> void =
    if n > 0 then (!res := n * !res; loop(n-1)) else ()
  val () = loop (n)
in
  !res
end // end of [fact]
')

The function #dyncode("fact") is written in such a style as somewhat a direct
translation of the following C code:

#atscode('\
//
int
fact (int n) {
  int res = 1 ;
  while (n > 0) { res = n * res; n = n - 1; } ;
  return (res) ;
}
//
')

The ATS program above is not accepted any more: it creates a reference on
heap and a non-linear closure-function, neither of which is allowed
without GC. (In earlier versions of ATS2, #dyncode("res") was a
heap-allocated reference that became garbage after a call to
#dyncode("fact") returned.)
On the other hand, the
variable #dyncode("res") in the C implementation of #dyncode("fact") is
stack-allocated (or it can even be mapped to a machine register), and there
is no generated garbage after a call to #dyncode("fact") returns. A
translation of the C implementation in ATS that is faithful to it makes use of
a stack-allocated variable, which is passed by reference to the loop:

#dats2xhtml('\
fun fact
  (n: int): int = let
  fun loop (n: int, res: &int >> _): void =
    if n > 0 then (res := n * res; loop(n-1, res)) else ()
  var res: int = 1
  val () = loop (n, res)
in
  res
end // end of [fact]
')

A proper translation, however, does not use any variable at all:

#dats2xhtml('\
fun fact2
  (n: int): int = let
  fun loop (n: int, res: int): int =
    if n > 0 then loop (n-1, n * res) else res
  // end of [loop]
in
  loop (n, 1)
end // end of [fact2]
')

")#comment("para")

#para("\

Unless strong justification can be given, making extensive use of
references (or even variables) is often a sure sign of poor coding style.

")#comment("para")

#para("\

#emphasis("References and Variables")
Both of the following lines create a reference from a statically allocated
variable. The variable #dyncode("myvar") is no longer available after its
at-view proof is consumed by #dyncode("ref_make_viewptr"):

#dats2xhtml('\
var myvar: int = 0
val myref = ref_make_viewptr{int} (view@(myvar) | addr@(myvar))
')

It should be interesting to
observe that both #dyncode("myvar") and #dyncode("myref") are just the same
pointer in C but they are the reification of fundamentally different
concepts in ATS: the former is a linear variable while the latter is a
non-linear reference.

")#comment("para")

#para("\

Please find #mycodelink("chap_reference.dats", "on-line") the entirety of the
code used in this chapter.\

")#comment("para")

</chapter><!--"reference"-->

